.TH SCRUBMAC 1 2026-10-04 "scrubmac 3.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
scrubmac \- update and clean your dev tools with one command
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B scrubmac
.RB [ \-n | \-\-dry\-run ]
.RB [ \-q | \-\-quiet ]
.RB [ \-\-update\-only | \-\-clean\-only ]
.RB [ \-\-skip
.IR cleaner ]
.RB [ \-\-measure ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RI [ cleaner .\|.\|.]
.br
.B scrubmac
.I command
.RI [ args ]
.br
.B scrubmac
.RB [ \-h | \-\-help | \-V | \-\-version ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B scrubmac
runs a set of drop-in "cleaner" scripts, each of which updates and cleans one
development tool (Homebrew, npm, uv, Claude Code, Docker, ...). Each cleaner
runs in its own process, with stdin from
.I /dev/null
and a time limit; a failing cleaner never stops the rest. Cleaners whose tool
is not installed skip themselves. A summary table ends every run, and every
real run leaves a log.
.PP
Safety doctrine: scrubmac never runs
.B sudo
itself (Homebrew may, to upgrade a pkg-based cask in a run you start in a
terminal), never touches user data \(em only updates, regenerable caches,
and Homebrew's removal of unneeded dependencies and old formula versions.
Opt-in cleaners
(docker, xcode, go, rubygems) are disabled until explicitly enabled. Updates
honor a supply-chain cooldown (default 7 days) wherever it can be enforced.
See
.I docs/security.md
for the threat model.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B run \fR[\fIcleaner\fR ...]
Run cleaners \(em the default command: without names, every enabled cleaner;
with names, exactly those (even disabled ones).
.TP
.B list \fR[\fB\-\-names\fR]
Show every cleaner: state (enabled/disabled), default, whether its tool is
present, source (built-in or yours), and summary.
.B \-\-names
prints names only (for shell completion; it has no side effects).
.TP
.B status \fR[\fIcleaner\fR ...]
Read-only report: each cleaner's cache sizes and outdated packages. Changes
nothing, does not take the run lock, and always shows cleaner output (even
with QUIET=1).
.TP
.B doctor
Environment and security report: install mode, settings whose values differ
from their defaults, unwritable config or state dirs, cleaner-file audit
(with the reason a file would be refused, and non-executable files of
yours), tools and how they were installed, schedule and crontab (including
crontab entries without a usable PATH, and scrubmac scheduled by both cron
and launchd), last run, the run lock, network and power, a PATH audit
(\(oq.\(cq and relative entries, world-writable directories), the
launcher on PATH, and dangling launcher links.
.TP
.B configure
(Re)run the interactive setup wizard. Every screen starts from the current
configuration; settings it does not manage are kept. Offered automatically on
the first interactive run.
.TP
.B enable \fIcleaner\fR ...
Turn cleaners on (recorded in
.IR ~/.config/scrubmac/enabled ).
Names may also be given as a comma-separated list.
.TP
.B disable \fIcleaner\fR ...
Turn cleaners off (recorded in
.IR ~/.config/scrubmac/disabled ).
.TP
.B config \fR[\fBget\fR|\fBset\fR|\fBunset\fR|\fBpath\fR|\fBkeys\fR] [\fIKEY\fR [\fIVALUE\fR]]
With no arguments, list every setting with its value and where it comes from
(flag, env, config, default). The other forms read, validate-and-write, or
remove a key, keeping comments and other lines intact; arguments pass
through as given, so a value may begin with a dash.
.B get
of a key that is neither a setting nor in the file exits 1;
.B set
of a key one edit away from a built-in key (a likely typo) is refused with
a suggestion;
.B keys
prints every key name, for shell completion.
.TP
.B schedule \fR[\fBdaily\fR [\fIHH:MM\fR] | \fBweekly\fR [\fIDAY\fR] [\fIHH:MM\fR] | \fBstatus\fR | \fBoff\fR]
Run scrubmac automatically through a per-user launchd agent
.RI ( ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.github.aviral2552.scrubmac.plist )
that carries the current PATH. DAY is a day name (mon \(en sun, or the
full name); without one, weekly runs on Monday; without HH:MM, at 09:00. A
run missed while the Mac slept happens at the next wake. With no argument,
or
.BR status ,
it reports the schedule and whether launchd has it loaded \(em and, when it
is not loaded or its launcher is gone, the exact command that recreates
it;
.B off
removes it.
.TP
.B last \fR[\fB\-\-json\fR]
Print the log of the most recent run (or, with
.BR \-\-json ,
its JSON record).
.TP
.B update \fR[\fB\-\-check\fR]
Update scrubmac itself. Git installs follow release tags by default: the
newest tag of the form vX.Y.Z that is a fast-forward from the installed copy
and, when the installed copy's
.I share/allowed_signers
pins keys, signed by one of them (newer tags that fail either test are
skipped with a warning; tags withdrawn upstream are pruned). Homebrew
installs run
.BR "brew upgrade" .
.B \-\-check
only reports what would change.
.TP
.B version
Print the version (and commit for git installs).
.TP
.B help
Show the help page.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Print every mutating command without executing it; read-only previews (such
as
.BR "brew upgrade \-\-dry\-run" )
do run, to show the real impact, and so do scrubmac's own first-run setup
and one-time migrations.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Print one result line per cleaner instead of its output; a failing
cleaner's output is still shown in full.
.TP
.B \-\-update\-only
Update tools; leave caches alone.
.TP
.B \-\-clean\-only
Clean caches; change no versions.
.TP
.BI \-\-skip " cleaner"
Leave a cleaner out of this run (repeatable; comma lists allowed).
.TP
.B \-\-measure
Report the space each cleaner frees (slower).
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the run's summary as JSON on stdout; all other output goes to stderr.
For runs,
.B status
and
.B last
only.
.TP
.B \-\-scheduled
Unattended run, as started by
.BR "scrubmac schedule" :
never interactive, honors ON_BATTERY and MIN_HOURS_BETWEEN_RUNS, and
notifies on failure.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show the help page.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version.
.PP
An option that would change what a command does, or that it cannot honor,
is an error, not silently ignored (for example
.B \-\-dry\-run
with
.BR update ,
which has
.B \-\-check
instead);
.B \-q
and
.B \-n
are accepted and ignored by read-only commands.
.SH SETTINGS
Settings live in
.I ~/.config/scrubmac/config
as KEY=value lines (values limited to A\-Z a\-z 0\-9 . _ / \-), and each can
be overridden for one run as
.BI CMM_ KEY
in the environment. Flags beat the environment, which beats the config file,
which beats the defaults. A malformed line is ignored and reported; a value
of the wrong type is reported and replaced by the default.
.TP
.B COOLDOWN_DAYS
supply-chain cooldown in days, 0 = off (default 7)
.TP
.B QUIET
1 = hide cleaner output unless it fails (default 0)
.TP
.B COLOR
auto, always, or never (default auto)
.TP
.B TIMEOUT
per-cleaner time limit in seconds, 0 = none (default 3600)
.TP
.B APP_UPDATES
Homebrew cask upgrades: interactive (only when run from a terminal),
always, or never (default interactive)
.TP
.B NOTIFY
desktop notification after unattended runs: failures, always, or never
(default failures)
.TP
.B ON_BATTERY
scheduled runs on battery power: run or skip (default run)
.TP
.B MIN_HOURS_BETWEEN_RUNS
scheduled runs skip when a full run succeeded within N hours (default 0)
.TP
.B LOG_KEEP
run logs to keep, at least 1 (default 20)
.TP
.B MEASURE
1 = measure space freed per cleaner (default 0)
.TP
.B UPDATE_CHANNEL
release or branch, for git installs (default release)
.TP
.B DERIVEDDATA_AGE_DAYS
xcode: purge DerivedData Xcode has not used for N days (default 30)
.TP
.B DEVICESUPPORT_AGE_DAYS
xcode: purge device-support folders older than N days (default 90)
.TP
.B HOMEBREW_DOCTOR
homebrew: run brew doctor and brew missing (default 1)
.TP
.B DOCKER_KEEP_HOURS
docker: keep build cache used within N hours (default 168)
.TP
.B MISE_PRUNE
mise: also remove tool versions no config file uses (default 0)
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
every cleaner succeeded or was skipped (a scheduled run that skipped itself
also exits 0)
.TP
.B 1
at least one cleaner failed, timed out, or was refused by the
execution-safety guard; also
.B config get
of an unknown key,
.B last \-\-json
before any run, an
.B update
that failed or was refused, and a
.B schedule
that launchd could not load
.TP
.B 2
usage or environment error (for example an unwritable state directory),
including a concurrent run holding the lock
.TP
.B 130
interrupted (a partial summary is printed once cleaners have started)
.PP
Individual cleaners exit 0 (ok), 75 (skipped), or anything else (failed).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.scrubmac/
the installed application (git installs)
.TP
.I ~/.config/scrubmac/config
KEY=value configuration \(em parsed, never executed
.TP
.I ~/.config/scrubmac/disabled
cleaners you turned off, one per line
.TP
.I ~/.config/scrubmac/enabled
cleaners you turned on, one per line
.TP
.I ~/.config/scrubmac/cleaners.d/
your own cleaners; a same-named cleaner overrides the built-in
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/scrubmac/
run logs, the last-run JSON record, the last-success stamp, the run lock (a
symlink naming the holder's pid and start time), and
.I launchd.log
(what scheduled runs printed; kept to about 512 KB plus one
.I .old
generation)
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.BI CMM_ KEY
override any setting for one run (see SETTINGS)
.TP
.B CMM_OFFLINE
1 or 0: force offline/online instead of probing the routing table
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
the configuration lives in
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/scrubmac
.TP
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
logs, the last-run record and the lock live in
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/scrubmac
.TP
.B CMM_STATE_DIR
relocate logs, the last-run record, and the lock (beats XDG_STATE_HOME)
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
disable colored output
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Preview everything without executing:
.B scrubmac \-\-dry\-run
.TP
Run only the Homebrew cleaner:
.B scrubmac homebrew
.TP
Free space now, upgrade nothing:
.B scrubmac \-\-clean\-only
.TP
Opt in to the Docker cache pruner:
.B scrubmac enable docker
.TP
Run every Monday at 09:00:
.B scrubmac schedule weekly
.SH SEE ALSO
The
.I docs/
directory of the source tree, and the
.UR https://github.com/aviral2552/scrubmac
project page
.UE .
